{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001267","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 9 candidates are direct matches to ankylosing spondylitis by trait_efo/trait_reported. For a generic disease target, the most endpoint-faithful options are the case/control-style models with phenotyping_reported="Ankylosing spondylitis" (e.g., PGS001267, PGS001876, PGS002089, and the Taiwanese EAS models), while the time-to-event model (PGS001268) is somewhat down-ranked per the provided policy when the target is not explicitly time-to-event.

Among endpoint-faithful options, PGS001267 has the clearest PRS-comparable performance package reported in the context: it provides both PGS-only AUROC (0.7265) and full-model AUROC (0.7433 with incremental AUROC 0.1299) in a European validation (n=67,425) with basic covariates (age, sex, array type, PCs). Its PGS-only R2 (0.0988) is also below the provided liability-scale heritability ceiling (~0.399), which is internally plausible as a sanity check. Competing UKB portability-framework scores (PGS001876, PGS002089) lack AUROC/R2 and only report partial-r, making cross-candidate comparison weaker from the visible evidence. The Taiwanese EAS models report higher full-model AUROC (~0.76–0.79) but have unknown covariates and a much smaller validation sample (n=1,298), so their apparent AUC advantage is less comparable/reliable given the policy guidance.

Limitation: PGS001267 comes from a pan-trait UKB snpnet framework paper (explicitly cautioned in the domain policy), and there is no clearly disease-focused multi-cohort alternative in the provided set with comparable PRS-only metrics; this framework-origin concern prevents a high-confidence/high-quality designation despite the strong and well-specified metric/validation metadata."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005271",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_efo = Hashimoto's thyroiditis) and share the same reported/phenotyped endpoint family (phenotyping_reported: lymphocytic thyroiditis) with the same publication context and the same validation sample size (n=94,651) and evaluation ancestry label (MAE) across African American or Afro-Caribbean, Hispanic or Latin American, European—so endpoint/validation/transportability evidence does not clearly separate them. Performance evidence is limited to a single reported AUROC per model, but it is a full-model AUROC with covariates listed as 'Unknown', which reduces comparability and makes the small AUROC differences hard to interpret. Given similar endpoint fidelity, PGS005271 is selected as best supported because it has the strongest available PRS-specific signal among the set via effect size (OR per SD ~1.55, higher than the others) while also having a comparable full-model AUROC (0.630 vs 0.639 for PGS005270) and the same validation support. Confidence is low because covariates are unknown, only full-model AUROC is provided (no PGS-only/incremental metrics), and the top AUROC model (PGS005270) is very close in discrimination, leaving unresolved ambiguity."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005220",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) are present. Among the OSA-labeled models, PGS005220 is from a disease-focused publication (\"Polygenic scores for obstructive sleep apnoea...\") with multi-cohort development (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP) and a reasonably large validation sample (n=21,975) across multiple ancestries, supporting endpoint relevance and transportability. Its reported full-model AUROC is 0.79, but because covariates include BMI (and no PGS-only or incremental metrics are provided), that AUROC is likely not PRS-comparable and should not be used as decisive evidence; still, the effect size (OR 1.53, 95% CI 1.39–1.68) provides supportive PRS-specific signal. PGS005219 shows the same AUROC and validation size with a stronger OR (1.98), but its label indicates BMI-adjusted phenotype; given the target is generic OSA and the context does not clarify whether BMI-adjusted or unadjusted is preferred, this creates endpoint ambiguity. Other OSA candidates (e.g., PGS003857/PGS003858) report AUROC=0.77 but are based on very sparse genome-wide significant SNP sets and also include BMI as a covariate, while PGS003479 has weak/unclear phenotype metadata. Overall, PGS005220 is preferred on disease-focus + multi-cohort support, but confidence is limited by covariate-heavy performance reporting and ambiguity between BMI-adjusted vs unadjusted OSA endpoints among the top candidates."
}
